    "suggested_action": "replace_ja_expression",
    "reason": "While '高貴' (kōki) can mean 'expensive' in the context of something being precious, noble, or of high status (e.g., '高貴な宝石' - precious/expensive gem), its primary meaning is 'noble' or 'high-class'. It is not the natural or common word a learner would use for general 'expensive' in daily life (e.g., 'expensive car', 'expensive meal'). The English lemma 'expensive' is broad, referring simply to high cost, whereas '高貴' implies inherent value or status. Therefore, the Japanese expression is semantically too narrow for the general concept.",
